<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fez</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fez is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, founded in the late eighth century and long one of the major centres of Islamic learning and trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> conquered it during its expansion northward in the eleventh century, absorbing it into its realm.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abroad, they kept strained relations with the Ibadi Rustamids of Tahert, tense relations with the Zaydi Shi'ite Idrisids of <b>Fez</b>, and a hostile stance towards the Umayyads of Córdoba.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Andalusian traditions laid the architectural and artistic foundations that the Almohad and Marinid dynasties would develop further, producing the distinctive Hispano-Moresque aesthetic visible in monuments such as the Qubba Ba'adiyyin in Marrakesh, the Great Mosque of Tlemcen and the Qarawiyyin Mosque in <b>Fez</b>, which Ali ibn Yusuf enlarged.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…according to tradition he had notables of Toledo massacred in 797, and in 818 he crushed the Revolt of the Arrabal, a rising of the southern suburb of Córdoba, razed the quarter and expelled its inhabitants, some of whom settled in <b>Fez</b> and others who later seized Crete.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Idris I had founded <b>Fez</b> as a Berber market town; Idris II developed it, moved his government there in 809 and founded the settlement of al-'Aliya, and welcomed two waves of Arab immigration, one from Córdoba in 818 and another from Aghlabid Tunisia in 824, which gave Fez…</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Uthman I and Muhammad I the tribe regrouped and built its authority over the rural tribes around Taza, <b>Fez</b> and Ksar el-Kebir.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early sixteenth century Morocco was divided: the Wattasid sultans at <b>Fez</b> controlled only the north, local lords and religious brotherhoods held much of the rest, and the Portuguese had seized a chain of Atlantic ports including Safi, Azemmour and Agadir, then known as Santa Cruz do…</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
